### 2. Syscall Dispatcher with Timing (`kernel/syscall.c`)

**File:** `kernel/syscall.c`

**Key Function:**
```c
void
syscall(void)
{
  int num;
  struct proc *p = myproc();
  uint64 args[6];

  num = p->trapframe->a7;  // Syscall number from register a7
  
  if(num > 0 && num < NELEM(syscalls) && syscalls[num]) {
    // Step 1: Save arguments from user registers
    args[0] = p->trapframe->a0;
    args[1] = p->trapframe->a1;
    args[2] = p->trapframe->a2;
    args[3] = p->trapframe->a3;
    args[4] = p->trapframe->a4;
    args[5] = p->trapframe->a5;
    
    // ========== TIMING START ==========
    p->syscall_start = ticks;  // Record kernel timer before syscall
    
    // Step 2: Execute the actual syscall
    p->trapframe->a0 = syscalls[num]();
    
    // Step 3: Calculate duration
    p->syscall_end = ticks;    // Record kernel timer after syscall
    p->syscall_duration = p->syscall_end - p->syscall_start;
    // ========== TIMING END ==========
    
    // Step 4: Conditionally print trace
    if(p->trace_mask & (1 << num)) {
      // Check if timing display is enabled
      if(p->trace_timing) {
        print_trace_with_timing(p, num, args, (int)p->trapframe->a0, p->syscall_duration);
      } else {
        print_trace(p, num, args, (int)p->trapframe->a0);
      }
    }
  } else {
    printf("%d %s: unknown sys call %d\n", p->pid, p->name, num);
    p->trapframe->a0 = -1;
  }
}
```

**How Timing Works:**
1. `ticks` is a global variable incremented by timer interrupt (~10ms per tick)
2. Before syscall: `p->syscall_start = ticks` captures current time
3. After syscall: `p->syscall_end = ticks` captures end time
4. Duration: `p->syscall_duration = p->syscall_end - p->syscall_start`

**Timing Examples:**
- `[0 ticks]` = syscall completed in < 10ms (e.g., write, getpid, fork)
- `[1 ticks]` = syscall took 10-20ms (e.g., file open, exec)
- `[171 ticks]` = syscall took ~1.71 seconds (e.g., read from keyboard waiting for input)

