### 3. Trace Output Function (`kernel/syscall.c`)

**File:** `kernel/syscall.c`

**Without Timing:**
```c
static void
print_trace(struct proc *p, int num, uint64 args[6], int ret)
{
  // Print: "PID: syscall_name(args) -> ret"
  printf("%d: %s(", p->pid, syscall_names[num]);
  
  // Print arguments based on syscall type
  switch(num) {
  case SYS_read:
    printf("fd=%d, buf=0x%x, count=%d", (int)args[0], (int)args[1], (int)args[2]);
    break;
  case SYS_write:
    printf("fd=%d, buf=0x%x, count=%d", (int)args[0], (int)args[1], (int)args[2]);
    break;
  // ... more cases ...
  }
  
  printf(") -> %d\n", ret);  // Just return value, no timing
}
```

**With Timing:**
```c
static void
print_trace_with_timing(struct proc *p, int num, uint64 args[6], int ret, uint64 duration)
{
  // Same as print_trace, but adds timing at the end
  printf("%d: %s(", p->pid, syscall_names[num]);
  
  // ... same argument printing ...
  
  printf(") -> %d [%d ticks]\n", ret, (int)duration);  // Add [ticks] here!
}
```

**Output Examples:**

Without timing:
```
2: read(fd=0, buf=0x4f1f, count=1) -> 1
3: write(fd=1, buf="hello", count=5) -> 5
```

With timing:
```
2: read(fd=0, buf=0x4f1f, count=1) -> 1 [171 ticks]    ← Waited for input
3: write(fd=1, buf="hello", count=5) -> 5 [0 ticks]    ← Fast operation
```

